          prompt: 'A data set has a mean of 30. Every value in it is then multiplied by 4. State the new mean and what happens to the standard deviation, justifying each answer, and contrast this with what would happen if 4 were ADDED to every value instead.',
          answer: 'Multiplying every value by 4 multiplies the total by 4 while the count is unchanged, so the new mean is 4 × 30 = 120. Every deviation from the mean is also multiplied by 4, so the standard deviation is multiplied by 4 as well — the data is stretched out. ADDING 4 to every value is different: the mean rises by 4, to 34, but every value and the mean move together, so every deviation is unchanged and the standard deviation does not change at all.',
          explanation: 'The contrast is the point. Scaling stretches the data, so it changes both the centre and the spread; shifting slides the data, so it changes the centre only. The reasoning marks are for treating the two operations as different in kind rather than as two similar-looking arithmetic changes.',
